          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which enzyme is the primary trigger that initiates the cascade of premature zymo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which enzyme is the primary trigger that initiates the cascade of premature zymogen activation within the pancreas in acute pancreatitis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lipase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lipase acts downstream in the cascade, causing fat necrosis in surrounding tissue. It is activated by trypsin and is not the initiating enzyme itself.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Trypsin</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Trypsin activates prematurely within the pancreas and triggers the cascade, switching on other dormant zymogens such as lipase and elastase, leading to autodigestion of the gland.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Amylase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Amylase is a serum marker used in the diagnosis of pancreatitis. It does not initiate the intrapancreatic autodigestive cascade.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Elastase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Elastase damages blood vessel walls and contributes to haemorrhagic necrosis, but it is activated downstream by trypsin — it is not the primary trigger.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the autodigestive mechanism: trypsin is the primary trigger that activates all other zymogens within the pancreas, initiating the cascade that leads to gland destruction."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Trypsin is the key — once it activates prematurely inside the gland, it unlocks the entire destructive enzymatic cascade."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
