          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 55-year-old man presents with acute epigastric pain radiating to the back. His</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 55-year-old man presents with acute epigastric pain radiating to the back. His serum lipase is five times the upper limit of normal. According to the revised Atlanta classification, which finding would classify his pancreatitis as severe?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum lipase more than ten times the upper limit of normal</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The height of enzyme elevation does not predict severity. A very high lipase does not mean a more severe attack — management decisions are driven by clinical severity grading, not enzyme numbers.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Organ failure persisting beyond 48 hours</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The revised Atlanta classification defines severe acute pancreatitis as organ failure that persists beyond 48 hours. This carries a high mortality and mandates intensive-care-level support.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pancreatic oedema visible on contrast CT at admission</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Oedema indicates interstitial (oedematous) pancreatitis — the milder, more common morphological form. Early CT is also discouraged because necrosis takes 72–96 hours to become apparent.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CRP above 100 mg/L at 24 hours</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CRP is a useful severity marker, but the threshold suggesting severe disease is above 150 mg/L at 48 hours, not 100 at 24 hours. It is a prognostic indicator, not the Atlanta classification criterion for severity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: the revised Atlanta classification grades severity by organ failure duration — transient organ failure (<48 h) is moderately severe; persistent organ failure (>48 h) is severe."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Persistent organ failure beyond 48 hours is the defining criterion — grade early, because the first hours are when aggressive intervention changes outcomes."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
