          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which feature most reliably distinguishes a functioning pancreatic neuroendocrin</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which feature most reliably distinguishes a functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour from a non-functioning one?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Functioning tumours are always benign, whereas non-functioning tumours are always malignant</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is incorrect. Malignancy potential varies by tumour type, not by functioning status. Gastrinomas, for instance, are functioning tumours that are frequently malignant.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Functioning tumours present early with a recognisable hormonal syndrome; non-functioning tumours present late by mass effect or metastasis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A functioning tumour secretes an active hormone whose clinical syndrome — such as Whipple's triad in insulinoma or Zollinger-Ellison syndrome in gastrinoma — announces the tumour early, often before any mass is detectable on imaging.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Functioning tumours arise from exocrine acinar cells, whereas non-functioning tumours arise from islet cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both functioning and non-functioning PNETs arise from the endocrine islet cells of the pancreas. The distinction is hormonal activity, not cell of origin.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Functioning tumours are found exclusively in the head of the pancreas</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PNETs can occur throughout the pancreas regardless of functioning status. While gastrinomas favour the gastrinoma triangle near the head, insulinomas and other PNETs may arise anywhere in the gland.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key distinction is hormonal activity: functioning tumours produce a recognisable clinical syndrome that leads to early detection, whereas non-functioning tumours are hormonally silent and present late."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The hormone drives the clinical picture — that is why functioning tumours are caught early, while non-functioning ones are discovered late by mass effect."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
