          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with confirmed Zollinger-Ellison syndrome presents with multiple refra</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with confirmed Zollinger-Ellison syndrome presents with multiple refractory peptic ulcers. What is the most important immediate management priority?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Urgent pancreatic resection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Resection may follow once the gastrinoma is localised, but it is not the immediate priority. Controlling acid hypersecretion comes first to halt ulcer progression and prevent life-threatening complications.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Somatostatin analogue infusion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Somatostatin analogues such as octreotide are used for receptor-positive tumours in advanced disease. They are not the first-line treatment for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>High-dose proton pump inhibitors</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. High-dose PPIs are the immediate priority — they halt dangerous gastric acid overproduction, allow ulcers to heal, and rapidly change the patient's clinical situation before localisation and resection are pursued.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Endoscopic ultrasound-guided biopsy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>EUS is a localisation tool used after biochemical confirmation. In an acutely symptomatic patient with refractory ulcers, controlling acid hypersecretion takes precedence over further investigation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, the immediate priority is high-dose proton pump inhibitors to control dangerous acid overproduction. Localisation and resection follow."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. In ZE syndrome, high-dose PPIs are what change the patient's situation quickly — surgical planning follows once the tumour is localised."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
